{"id":"ba-earnings-q3-2026","title":"BA earnings — Boeing, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-10-27","html":"<p>Management sees a record order book and a slow, steady climb in output, and the thing that could spoil it is a factory that still cannot build 737 wings fast enough to turn those orders into cash.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-10-27. Next report: not found. Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> Stronger. Record backlog of $745.4B and 300 net commercial orders in Q3.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> Cut in effect. No formal guidance, but the cash targets moved out about a year: free cash flow of about $2B in 2026, $3B in 2027, $6B in 2028 and $10B in 2029.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> Production stability: 737 wings, the climb to 52 a month, 777X certification by mid-2027.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> Leans down. A daily close above $185 ends that.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $24,188M, up 4%, against about $23.9B expected: a beat.</li><li>Core loss per share $0.34 against a $0.25 loss expected: a miss. Core is Boeing's adjusted figure. The GAAP loss was smaller at $0.26, so do not set the GAAP number against the estimate.</li><li>Free cash flow $464M.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Backlog:</strong> Commercial $611.8B (over 6,300 airplanes), defense a record $101B, services $33B.</li><li><strong>737:</strong> Still stabilizing at 47 a month, expected to take into next year. 52 a month is targeted for the second half of 2027. 2026 deliveries will fall short of the 500 target.</li><li><strong>787:</strong> Stable at 8 a month; the move to 10 starts this year and is paced by engine deliveries. 2026 deliveries of 90 to 100, nearer the low end.</li><li><strong>777X:</strong> First delivery still expected in 2027. The financial plan assumes certification by mid-2027.</li><li><strong>Cash:</strong> The CFO called it roughly a one-year shift to the right, with the slope and endpoint unchanged.</li><li><strong>Airlines:</strong> None has replanned orders so far, though growth orders could soften.</li><li><strong>Tone:</strong> &quot;I'm confident we're on the right track.&quot; (Kelly Ortberg, President and CEO, earnings call).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li>737 rate stability at 47 a month, with wings the main constraint.</li><li>777X certification timing: mid-2027 is built into the cash plan.</li><li>Engine supply for the 787, holding the move from 8 to 10 a month.</li><li>Softer airline growth orders if fuel stays high.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-production-stability-both-ways\">The big worry: production stability, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Wings catching up. Boeing said in September it would add wing capacity (company feature, Sep 16).</li><li>The new Everett line. Leeham News reports it adds five a month on top of Renton's 47, with deliveries expected to start early 2027.</li><li>Certification is clearing: the Max 7 was certified in early August and the Max 10 in October (Leeham).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Deliveries stuck near today's pace. Leeham counts 367 737s delivered in nine months, about 41 a month, well under the 47 being built toward.</li><li>The regulator. The FAA capped output at 38 a month after January 2024, lifted that in October 2025 and reviewed the line before approving 47 (Seattle Times via Aviation Pros). Higher rates need the Everett line's production certification.</li><li>A 777X slip past mid-2027. Boeing took another charge on the program in Q3.</li><li>Airlines trimming growth. Delta is holding Q4 seat growth under 2% and paying about $4.25 a gallon; United pays about $4.80.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The order book is not the problem: over 6,300 airplanes.</li><li>The balance sheet is the weak spot: $45.9B of debt against $20.7B of cash and securities, with the 10-year near 5%.</li><li>Against peers, Lockheed and RTX raised free cash flow this quarter. Boeing pushed its $10B out to 2029.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li>2027 capital spending about $5B, about $1B higher, for Charleston, St. Louis and the Navy F/A-XX fighter; about $4B a year after.</li><li>Debt flat at $45.9B; cash and securities $20.7B.</li><li>Dividends and buybacks: not discussed.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Kelly Ortberg, President and CEO since 2024 (2026 proxy). Not a founder.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Jay Malave, since Aug 15, 2025, formerly Lockheed Martin's finance chief. He replaced Brian West (CFO Dive).</li><li><strong>Chair:</strong> Steven Mollenkopf, independent chair since 2024. Boeing's by-laws require an independent chair, so the roles are split.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> No. The proxy says a board committee oversees senior management succession.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> Dual-class shares or a controlling holder: not found on the pages opened.</li><li><strong>Recent changes:</strong> Brad Tilden joined the board in December 2025 (proxy). No executive departure in the last 12 months was found.</li><li><strong>Open oversight:</strong> A May 2025 non-prosecution agreement with the Department of Justice, which the board's audit committee reviews. NPR reported in November 2025 that a judge dismissed the criminal charge over the 737 Max crashes; Boeing agreed to pay or invest a further $1.1B and may pick its own compliance consultant, and some families planned to appeal. FAA review of each 737 rate increase continues.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> Less on a person than on the factory. The CEO and CFO are both recent hires and the chair is independent.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"BA\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"BA · daily · my levels (not yours) chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">BA · daily · my levels (not yours)</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"119.0\" y2=\"119.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"10.3\" x2=\"10.3\" y1=\"132.6\" y2=\"143.0\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"8.9\" y=\"138.2\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"1.4\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"14.9\" x2=\"14.9\" y1=\"124.3\" y2=\"153.4\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"137.4\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"7.4\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"19.6\" x2=\"19.6\" y1=\"147.9\" y2=\"169.4\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"18.1\" y=\"150.3\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"15.2\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"24.2\" x2=\"24.2\" y1=\"117.7\" y2=\"162.5\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"22.8\" y=\"118.2\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"44.1\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"28.8\" x2=\"28.8\" y1=\"111.2\" y2=\"136.7\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"27.4\" y=\"115.5\" width=\